{ignore}
<p><code>{can 'edit-post', $post}</code> asks a gate whether the user logged in may do
something, and renders its content when the answer is yes; the part after its own
<code>{else}</code>, or the <code>&#64;else&#64;</code> half, renders when it is no. The gates are
functions the application defines once, in a file of its <code>_lib/</code>:</p>

<pre><code>padGate ( 'edit-post', fn ( array $user, array $post ) =&gt; $user ['id'] === $post ['author'] );</code></pre>

<p>The first item is the ability, the others are what the gate is given after the user: an
expression each, the name of an array of the page as that array - and the name of an enclosing
loop as the row of its current occurrence, so <code>{posts}{can 'edit-post', $posts}</code>
asks about one post, not the list.</p>

<p><code>padGateBefore ( fn ( $user, $ability ) =&gt; ... )</code> hooks are asked first:
TRUE or FALSE decides, NULL leaves it to the gate - an admin who may do anything. A guest
reaches only a gate (or hook) whose user parameter takes NULL - <code>?array $user</code> or
<code>$user = NULL</code>; any other gate is not even asked and answers no. An ability nobody
defined is FALSE and, under the strict check, an error, so a misspelled name is reported
instead of silently hiding a button. <a href="?tag/cannot">{cannot}</a> turns the answer round;
<code>padCan</code>, <code>padCannot</code> and <code>padAuthorize</code> (403) ask from
PHP.</p>

<p>The gates of the examples here are in this application's
<code>_lib/canGates.php</code>: <code>read-post</code> takes a guest, <code>edit-post</code>
does not.</p>
{/ignore}
